  3.819264913256879*^9, 3.819264928962316*^9}, {3.94682300688763*^9, 
  3.9468230110296793`*^9}, {3.946902261458311*^9, 
  3.946902290583809*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"52e01fc9-95ca-4db1-820e-\
1392932475a4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The code here is inspired by and/or copied from David Soloveichik\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s CRNSimulator package (available from \
http://users.ece.utexas.edu/~soloveichik/crnsimulator.html) that consists of \
three files CRNSimulator.m, CRNSimulatorExtensions.m, and CRNSimulatorSSA.m \
that are usually placed in the same directory as your notebook and imported \
using ",
 StyleBox["SetDirectory[NotebookDirectory[]]; \
Get[\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]CRNSimulator.m\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ".  This is the proper way to do it, with a private package namespace, help \
messages and error messages and all that stuff.  However, I want you to see \
for yourself that there isn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t any mystery or magic, and that \
if you have a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]model of computation\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] of interest, you might be able to implement it in \
just a few lines of code. \n\nSo, here is a (re)implementation of Soloveichik\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s package, which is (mostly) compatible with his code."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.693186213042845*^9, 3.693186221185954*^9}, {
   3.69318630670045*^9, 3.693186327610448*^9}, {3.693186392702433*^9, 
   3.693186456169381*^9}, {3.693186668465563*^9, 3.6931866720684223`*^9}, {
   3.693246071236302*^9, 3.693246072876423*^9}, {3.693246143033906*^9, 
   3.693246180138476*^9}, {3.693246331425003*^9, 3.6932463400241632`*^9}, {
   3.693246374785656*^9, 3.693246425191392*^9}, {3.755918614061541*^9, 
   3.755918651329892*^9}, {3.755919302070119*^9, 3.755919335315695*^9}, {
   3.755925862342453*^9, 3.755925877523798*^9}, {3.755925912204608*^9, 
   3.7559259480113907`*^9}, {3.8192646508152657`*^9, 3.819264655844804*^9}, {
   3.8192646865191402`*^9, 3.819264744154022*^9}, {3.8255468832419853`*^9, 
   3.825547033642633*^9}, {3.8255470896207933`*^9, 3.825547089950441*^9}, {
   3.851041063448213*^9, 3.8510412348441553`*^9}, {3.88309409861235*^9, 
   3.883094124866208*^9}, {3.883094165611187*^9, 3.883094181296136*^9}, {
   3.91446813770247*^9, 3.9144681546092043`*^9}, {3.91446822386381*^9, 
   3.914468249047769*^9}, {3.946840069916431*^9, 3.9468401354337606`*^9}, {
   3.946840384500535*^9, 3.946840713634446*^9}, 3.980347600421364*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.9, 0.8],ExpressionUUID->"c3039927-239c-4b47-a333-e7e0fc23a0fc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]reaction system\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is a list \
of formal reactions (",
 StyleBox["rxn",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["revrxn",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " statements) together with initial conditions (",
 StyleBox["conc",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " statements).  You can think of the former as the CRN and the latter as the \
initial state.   If you have multiple copies of the same (or equivalent) \
reactions, effectively their rates add; for multiple statements specifying \
the same species concentration, again they add.\n\nIn StandardForm show ",
 StyleBox["rxn",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["revrxn",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " using standard chemical arrow notation.  Note that FullForm still \
preserves ",
 StyleBox["rxn",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["revrxn",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " so all pattern matching works.  The shown arrow expressions cannot be \
modified but can be copied and pasted."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.656291888224341*^9, 3.656291897098534*^9}, {
  3.665621499107088*^9, 3.665621507327568*^9}, {3.819264936968506*^9, 
  3.8192649389601183`*^9}, {3.946839786502255*^9, 3.946839822817588*^9}, {
  3.946840042618548*^9, 3.946840044718988*^9}, {3.9468407179654493`*^9, 
  3.946840809224617*^9}, {3.946840854838402*^9, 3.946840874891594*^9}, {
  3.9468968166556473`*^9, 3.946896896315568*^9}},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.9, 0.8],ExpressionUUID->"47aed07a-957d-430e-a922-5513822cecbc"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ClearAll", "[", 
   RowBox[{"rxn", ",", "revrxn", ",", "conc", ",", "t"}], "]"}], 
  "\[IndentingNewLine]"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Format", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"rxn", "[", 
     RowBox[{"rs_", ",", "ps_", ",", "k_"}], "]"}], ",", "StandardForm"}], 
   "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Interpretation", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Row", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"rs", ",", 
        RowBox[{"Overscript", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"RawBoxes", "[", "\"\<\[LongRightArrow]\>\"", "]"}], ",", 
          "k"}], "]"}], ",", "ps"}], "}"}], ",", "\"\< \>\""}], "]"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"rxn", "[", 
     RowBox[{"rs", ",", "ps", ",", "k"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Format", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"revrxn", "[", 
     RowBox[{"rs_", ",", "ps_", ",", "k1_", ",", "k2_"}], "]"}], ",", 
    "StandardForm"}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Interpretation", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Row", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"rs", ",", 
        RowBox[{"Underoverscript", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"RawBoxes", "[", "\"\<\[LongLeftRightArrow]\>\"", "]"}], 
          ",", "k2", ",", "k1"}], "]"}], ",", "ps"}], "}"}], ",", 
      "\"\< \>\""}], "]"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"revrxn", "[", 
     RowBox[{"rs", ",", "ps", ",", "k1", ",", "k2"}], "]"}]}], 
   "]"}]}]}], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.946839784794478*^9, {3.946839826107869*^9, 3.9468399082637043`*^9}, {
   3.946839957707117*^9, 3.946839985254603*^9}, {3.9468408384364357`*^9, 
   3.946840838762898*^9}, {3.946842094917124*^9, 3.946842150057418*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[1]:=",ExpressionUUID->"181ca780-f91c-4c3b-9398-b70e648c849d"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"rxn", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"A", "+", "B"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"2", "X"}], ",", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"revrxn", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"2", "A"}], "+", "X"}], ",", "AC", ",", "10", ",", "0.1"}], 
    "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"conc", "[", 
    RowBox[{"AC", ",", ".2"}], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"conc", "[", 
    RowBox[{"B", ",", ".3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9468412271167307`*^9, 3.946841269384911*^9}, {
  3.9468967766571083`*^9, 3.946896787568996*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[13]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9854b9de-3c53-43d0-b2e9-fe4cb0ab58a1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For this package, a model reaction system consists of an unordered set of \
reactions and concentration statements.  Species names are simply Mathematica \
symbols (i.e. variables) that have no values or rules assigned to them.  \
Thus, for example, the symbols \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]C\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] and \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]D\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
and \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]N\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] and \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]I\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] should not be used as \
species names, because Mathematica has built-in meanings for these terms.  \
Similarly, if your code assigns e.g. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"x", "=", "17"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7201e95c-b6d2-4a09-a6fa-25530d94c0dc"],
 ", then \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]x\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] cannot be used as a \
species name unless you first execute ",
 StyleBox["Clear[x]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ".  Once a reaction system has been defined, ",
 StyleBox["SimulateRxnsys[ ]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " builds the corresponding ODE equations for continuous deterministic \
mass-action chemical kinetics and performs the simulation.  The numerical \
integration of ODEs in Mathematica is an interpolating function that can be \
queried to obtain the value at a particular point.  These functions can be \
passed to ",
 StyleBox["Plot[ ]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " to draw the results, or they can be used in others ways to interrogate the \
simulation\[CloseCurlyQuote]s results.\n\nMathematica automatically colors \
symbols according to how they are defined; here you can see the uppercase \
single-letter symbols are blue when there are no definitions associated with \
it, but are black when they have defined (or pre-defined) meanings.  So you \
are safe to use the blue ones (so long as you don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t give \
them definitions) but not the black ones.  You\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll see also \
that lower-case are all blue.  Of course, in your CRNs you will often want to \
have species names that are longer, or even more complicated, like ",
 StyleBox["x[3,4]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", so long as they remain operationally inert."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.693184611752716*^9, 3.693184650341352*^9}, {
   3.693184697661479*^9, 3.693185050035988*^9}, {3.693186190008719*^9, 
   3.6931861999823914`*^9}, {3.693246464847949*^9, 3.6932464665119343`*^9}, {
   3.946841530855454*^9, 3.946841594296389*^9}, {3.946841808499853*^9, 
   3.9468418512467957`*^9}, {3.946841896131626*^9, 3.9468420275422792`*^9}, {
   3.946842080537857*^9, 3.946842090405548*^9}, {3.946842159420587*^9, 
   3.946842249489091*^9}, 3.9803476471549*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.9, 0.8],ExpressionUUID->"ad1f76d8-bed1-42bb-b83b-303fa760058c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "A", ",", "B", ",", "C", ",", "D", ",", "\[ExponentialE]", ",", "F", ",", 
   "G", ",", "H", ",", "\[ImaginaryI]", ",", "J", ",", "K", ",", "L", ",", 
   "M", ",", "N", ",", "O", ",", "P", ",", "Q", ",", "R", ",", "S", ",", "T", 
   ",", "U", ",", "V", ",", "W", ",", "X", ",", "Y", ",", "Z"}], 
  "}"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.946396020674485*^9},
 CellLabel->
